(window.PHASE_DATA=window.PHASE_DATA||{})[3]={"pages": [{"pdf": 132, "printed": 105, "ch": 0, "text": "PHASE 3\n\nClimate Economics: Discounting, Risk\nand Catastrophe\nChapters 14–18\n\nStern Review (Chapter 2 and Postscript); Weitzman (2009); Dietz and Stern (2015)"}, {"pdf": 133, "printed": 106, "ch": 14, "text": "CHAPTER 14\n\nStern Review, Chapter 2: Ethics, Discounting and Risk\nSource line\nNicholas Stern et al., Stern Review: The Economics of Climate Change (HM Treasury, 2006), Chapter 2, “Economics, ethics and climate change”, printed pp. 23–38, plus its Appendix 2A, “Ethical frameworks and intertemporal equity” (printed pp. 41 onward; the part I read most closely is 2A.2, the Ramsey rule and the survival table). In your PDF, printed page = PDF page −45 for these pages. Chapter 6 of the Review (the model run) is not in the folder as a separate reading; I cite it only where Chapter 2 and the Postscript describe it.\n\n14.1 Why this chapter is the one to master\nStern’s report is famous for a number: the claim that business-as-usual climate change would cost at least 5% of global consumption now and for ever, against about 1% of GDP to avoid the worst. The number was attacked mainly on one ground, the discount rate. Chapter 2 is where Stern states, in plain terms, the ethical choices that make the number what it is. Learn this chapter and you can see what the argument rests on, whoever makes it. For your thesis the chapter matters in three ways.\n• Any climate scenario that a bank’s risk team uses (NGFS in Phase 6) contains a hidden choice about how much the future counts. Chapter 2 shows where that choice lives.\n• Sections 2.5 and 2.6 restate, in an applied setting, the Knight–Keynes distinction between risk and uncertainty from Phase 1 (Chapters 1–3, Phase 1).\n• It is a model of how to write value judgements openly, which is useful when your own thesis makes assumptions about loss given default or about the time horizon of a stress test.\n\n14.2 The externality and the social cost of carbon (section 2.2,\npp. 24–28)\nStern begins with the textbook definition. Climate change is an externality: those who emit greenhouse gases impose costs on others, and on future generations, which they do not pay (p. 24). Stern then adds four features that make it unlike a local pollution problem (pp. 24–25): it is global (the damage does not depend on where the gas was emitted), long-lasting (the"}, {"pdf": 134, "printed": 107, "ch": 14, "text": "gases persist for decades to centuries), uncertain (about both the science and the economics), and non-marginal (large changes are possible, which affects how we do the sums). What a century does to a damage\n\nδ as an extinction hazard\nChance humanity still exists (%)\n\nPresent value of 100 of damage\n\n102\n\n101\n\n100\n\nDiscount rate ρ = δ + ηg (g=2%)\n2.1% (Stern: δ=0.1, η=1)\n3.1% (δ=0.1, η=1.5)\n4.5% (δ=1.5, η=1.5)\n5.5% (δ=1.5, η=2)\n\n100\n150\nYears from now\n\n100\n\nδ = 0.1%\n\nδ = 0.5%\nδ = 1.0%\nδ = 1.5%\n\n200\n\nYears from now\n\n100\n\nFigure 14.1. Left: what a damage of 100 occurring t years from now is worth today at four discount rates built from the Ramsey rule (my illustration, with growth g = 2% a year; the vertical scale is logarithmic). Right: the chance that humanity is still here after t years if δ is read as a constant extinction hazard (the dots are the values in Stern’s Table 2A.1, in the appendix).\n\nThe social cost of carbon (SCC) and marginal abatement cost (MAC)\nThe social cost of carbon is the present value of the damage caused by one extra tonne of carbon dioxide (or its equivalent) emitted now. The marginal abatement cost is the cost of cutting one more tonne. Pigou’s rule says: abate until the two are equal (Figure 2.1 in the Review, p. 26). The tax that induces this is equal to the SCC at that point. Stern’s refinement, in Figure 2.2 (p. 27), is the point to remember: the SCC is not a single number; it depends on the path emissions will follow. If emissions stay high the world is hotter, and one more tonne does more damage (the damage function is convex), so the SCC is higher. Therefore the “right” price and the “right” abatement are decided together: a path, not a point. This is why the Review cannot just look up a carbon price. Remember The SCC is an outcome of the whole model: it depends on the damage function, the discount rate, the emissions path and the treatment of risk. When two studies report different SCCs the first question is never “whose science is better?” but “which of these four choices differs?”\n\n14.3 Ethics and the social welfare function (section 2.3, pp. 28–31)\nStern chooses the social welfare function approach: well-being is measured by the utility of consumption, and social welfare is the sum of utilities across people and across time. “Consumption” is meant broadly, “a broad range of goods and services that includes education, health and the environment” (p. 29–30 in the printed pages). The approach has costs and Stern lists them. Aggregation can conflict with rights-based ethics; different value judgements can rank the same outcomes differently; democratic aggregation has"}, {"pdf": 135, "printed": 108, "ch": 14, "text": "the difficulties explored by Arrow and Sen (footnote 7). Its merits are clarity and simplicity: because the value judgements are explicit numbers, you can vary them and see how the answer moves. This is the key methodological idea of the chapter: do not hide ethics, parameterise it and test sensitivity.\n\nCalibrating by thought experiment (Box 2.1, p. 30)\nHow do you decide how much a rupee to a poor person is worth relative to a rupee to a rich one? Stern’s device: suppose a policy gives a poor person an extra X and takes Y from a rich person; how much larger than X would Y have to be before you judge the policy to be a bad one? Your answer reveals the curvature of your utility function, a parameter called η (eta), the elasticity of marginal utility of consumption. Here is the translation:\n• η = 0: a rupee is a rupee to everyone; only the total matters.\n• η = 1: a 1% rise in consumption adds the same utility whoever you are (log utility); someone twice as rich values an extra rupee half as much.\n• η = 2: marginal utility falls with the square of consumption: someone twice as rich values an extra rupee a quarter as much. The same η also governs how we treat future generations who will be richer than us, and (in Weitzman, Chapter 16) how much we dislike risk. One parameter, three jobs: inequality aversion across people, inequality aversion across time, and risk aversion. That is worth a full line in your notes because it is why the debate over η is so heated. The footnote on valuing lives (p. 30, footnote 8)\n\nStern notes that if the money value of a life in country A is twice that in country B because income there is twice as high, we may value increments of income in A half as much as in B, and then “extra mortality would be valued in the same way for both countries”. In plain words: the welfare approach can avoid putting a lower price on a poor person’s life because she is poor. This answers a standard objection to the use of willingness-to-pay in climate damage estimates.\n\n14.4 Discounting (section 2.4, pp. 31–33, Box 2.2, and Appendix\n2A)\nThe two reasons to discount\nStern says that, in standard welfare economics, one extra unit of future consumption is worth less than one today for two reasons (p. 31).\n1. Growth. If consumption grows, people in the future are richer, and an extra unit matters less to a richer person. This depends on η and on the growth rate g.\n2. Pure time preference. People prefer good things sooner, because of impatience and because they might not be alive later. This is the parameter δ."}, {"pdf": 136, "printed": 109, "ch": 14, "text": "Stern accepts the first and nearly rejects the second. The central ethical stance of the Review, p. 31: “if a future generation will be present, we suppose that it has the same claim on our ethical attention as the current one”. The only reason for δ > 0 is the small chance that the world will not exist (a “meteorite”). The Ramsey rule, in one line ρ = δ + ηg ρ is the (consumption) discount rate; δ is pure time preference; η is the elasticity of marginal utility; g is the growth rate of consumption per person. (Appendix 2A.2, equation (8), writes this with ġ/c as the growth rate of consumption.) It is called after Frank Ramsey (1928), whom Stern quotes against pure time preference. The rule says: the weight on the future is lower when people are impatient, or when they are rich and getting richer and they dislike inequality.\n\nThe quotations\nStern marshals a distinguished group of economists against δ > 0 for ethical reasons (p. 31– 32): Ramsey called it “ethically indefensible” and arising “merely from the weakness of the imagination”; Pigou said it implies that “our telescopic faculty is defective”; Harrod called it a “human infirmity” and “a polite expression for rapacity and the conquest of reason by passion”; Solow said we should act as if the social rate of time preference were zero, while still discounting if the future is richer. Anand and Sen (2000) and Cline (1992) are cited as taking a similar view. A misreading to avoid Stern does not say that the discount rate is zero. With δ ≈ 0, ρ = ηg is still positive when consumption is growing. “Low pure time preference” does not mean “low discount rate”. The Appendix gives a case: with η = 1.5 and g = 2.5% the growth term alone is 3.75% (appendix 2A.2). The Review’s own central case sets η = 1 and δ = 0.1% (Chapter 6, quoted in the Postscript), so ρ is modest, but not zero.\n\nWhy δ = 0.1% and not zero: the extinction argument (Appendix, Table 2A.1)\nIf the only legitimate reason to discount is the chance that there will be no one to enjoy the consumption, then δ is a hazard rate, and the chance of surviving t years is e−δt . Table 14.1 shows what different δ imply. The point is to see the number as a probability of the end of the world and ask whether you really believe it. A δ of 1% implies a 63% chance that humanity does not survive the century. That is the argument that the commonly used values are too high to be read as an extinction risk. Stern gives one defensible reading of δ; the remainder of the appendix (and the Review’s critics) argues about whether other kinds of reasons should count."}, {"pdf": 137, "printed": 110, "ch": 14, "text": "δ (% a year) Survive 50 years Survive 100 years Survive 150 years\n0.1\n\n0.951\n\n0.905\n\n0.861\n\n0.5\n\n0.779\n\n0.607\n\n0.472\n\n1.0\n\n0.607\n\n0.368\n\n0.223\n\n1.5\n\n0.472\n\n0.223\n\n0.105\n\nTable 14.1. Survival probability implied by pure time preference read as an extinction hazard, e−δt . The 100-year column reproduces Stern’s Table 2A.1 (0.905, 0.607, 0.368, 0.223, all as given in the appendix); the 50- and 150-year columns are my arithmetic, which you can check by hand.\n\nArrow’s objection and Stern’s reply (appendix 2A.2)\nKenneth Arrow (1995) argued that δ = 0 together with a high elasticity would imply an implausibly high rate of saving: the present generation would be asked to sacrifice almost everything for descendants. Stern rebuts it in the appendix; I have not reproduced the rebuttal’s steps, because they are technical and I would only be paraphrasing from memory. For your purposes, remember that the objection exists, that it targets an abstract optimal-growth model, and that the answer is in Appendix 2A.2.\n\nMore than one generation or more than one person\nThe appendix also covers (a) changing population: whether to weigh total or average utility (the appendix discusses both; I do not give its verdict here), and (b) inequality within a generation: the theorem of Atkinson that with a concave utility function, a mean-preserving spread lowers welfare. The practical lesson: unequal damages (poor countries lose more) raise the aggregate cost of climate change relative to a world with the same average loss spread equally.\n\nConvergence\nFor the sums to be finite when we add up utility to infinity, we need δ large enough relative to growth: the condition is that (1 − η)g − δ < 0 (the Postscript’s technical annex gives this form). With η > 1 it holds automatically; with η = 1 it requires δ > 0 (I suspect, though the text I read does not say so, that this is a further reason for a small positive δ besides the meteorite argument).\n\n14.5 Risk and uncertainty (section 2.5, pp. 33–34)\nStern’s treatment of risk is short but it carries a full position.\n• Expected utility with subjective probability. Following Ramsey (1931), probabilities may be subjective; Bayes’ theorem updates them. This lets Stern apply the standard tools to a one-off event.\n• Risk and the discount rate are different things. Risk aversion comes from the curvature of utility (η), and does not need to be added as a separate “risk premium” on the discount rate. A project with a risky payoff is valued by taking expected utility, not by adding a few percentage points to the interest rate."}, {"pdf": 138, "printed": 111, "ch": 14, "text": "• The Knight–Keynes distinction is acknowledged, then set aside. Stern recognises that many probabilities cannot be known; he points to recent theory (Maccheroni and Klibanoff) on ambiguity aversion, and to Claude Henry’s discussion of the precautionary principle.\n• The key sentence: Quoting Henry (2006), “uncertainty should not be inflated and invoked as an alibi for inaction” (p. 34). “We now have a theory that can describe how to act.” My reading (labelled as such)\n\nStern’s treatment of uncertainty goes only one step beyond expected utility: it names the problem and says it should not paralyse policy. It does not say how to act when the probabilities themselves are unknown. That is where Weitzman (Chapter 16) takes over, and where the Knight–Keynes view of Phase 1 would add: you cannot compute your way out of it; you need judgement and institutions.\n\n14.6 Non-marginal decisions and the shape of the policy argument\n(sections 2.6–2.8)\nSection 2.6 (p. 34–35, Figure 2.3 in the printed numbering; the text calls it Figure 2.4, a small slip in the Review) says that a standard marginal cost-benefit calculation does not work when the decision changes the growth path of the economy. The picture has two curves of log income: one with mitigation that starts lower (costs now) and later pulls ahead; one without mitigation that starts higher and later falls (damages). The comparison is between two whole paths. Part III of the Review, Stern says, finds that “the losses from mitigation in the near future are strongly outweighed by the later gains in averted damage”. Section 2.7 (pp. 35–36) says that the first task of policy is a price on carbon (tax or trading), the second is support for research and deployment of technology, the third is the removal of barriers and the shaping of behaviour. It notes that, under risk, long-term quantity targets may be better than taxes. Section 2.8 (p. 37–38) is about international collective action; I did not read it closely. These are not central for your thesis, so I treat them as background.\n\n14.7 Common misreadings\nFour things people get wrong about Stern’s Chapter 2\n\n1. “Stern uses a zero discount rate.” No: δ ≈ 0.1% and ρ = δ + ηg > 0.\n2. “Stern’s result is just the choice of δ.” The Postscript’s Tables PA.2 and PA.3 (Chapter 15) show that raising δ to 1.5% cuts the loss from 10.9% to 3.3% in one case, but the loss is still above 1%. The choice of damage function and η matter as well.\n3. “The ethics section is philosophy, not economics.” The ethical parameters (δ, η) are in the formula that gives the number. They are the economics.\n4. “Stern ignores uncertainty.” It is central (Chapter 6 uses a probabilistic model);"}, {"pdf": 139, "printed": 112, "ch": 14, "text": "Section\n\nWhat it settles\n\nPages\n\n2.2 Externality\n\nClimate is a global, long-lived, uncertain, non-marginal externality; 24–28\nthe SCC depends on the emissions path.\n\n2.3 Ethics\n\nUse a social welfare function; make value judgements explicit; η 28–31\ncalibrated by thought experiment.\n\n2.4 Discounting\n\nEqual weight to future generations; δ only for the chance of extinc- 31–33\ntion; the Ramsey rule.\n\n2.5 Risk\n\nExpected utility; risk aversion lives in η; uncertainty is not an alibi 33–34\nfor inaction.\n\n2.6 Non-marginal\n\nCompare whole growth paths with and without mitigation.\n\n34–35\n\n2.7 Policy\n\nPrice carbon, support technology, shape behaviour.\n\n35–36\n\n2.8 Global action\n\nInternational collective action (not read closely).\n\n37–38\n\nTable 14.2. A map of Chapter 2 for quick reference.\n\nwhat he does not do is treat uncertainty about the probabilities themselves. That is Weitzman’s point.\n\n14.8 What it means for the thesis\nThis section is my inference, not Stern’s.\n• Horizon choice. A bank’s capital plan looks 3–10 years ahead. Stern’s argument is about centuries. The two meet only through the scenarios (NGFS): the near-term transition path is judged by how the long-term cost is valued. Your thesis, which is on capital adequacy, can accept the long-run ethics as given and concentrate on the transmission to bank balance sheets, but you should state that the scenarios embody this choice.\n• A template for stating assumptions. Stern’s practice of exposing δ and η and testing sensitivity is a model for the way your own thesis can expose assumptions such as the probability of default, loss given default and the scenario’s carbon price.\n• Risk and the discount rate are separate. When you discount future losses in a stress test you should not add a risk premium to the rate and also model the loss distribution; that would count risk twice. Do you need the original? For the ideas: no. This chapter contains them (equal weight to future generations, the Ramsey rule, the extinction reading of δ, risk via η, the Henry quotation). Read pp. 31–33 (Box 2.2) in the original if you plan to lecture on discounting: the quotations are best quoted from the page, and the wording of the ethical stance matters. Read Appendix 2A.2 (printed pp. 43–49) if you will defend Stern’s choice of δ in a viva: I have given the Arrow objection and the reply in outline only. Not needed for your thesis: sections 2.7 and 2.8, which concern policy design and"}, {"pdf": 140, "printed": 113, "ch": 14, "text": "international cooperation.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ14.1 List the four features that make climate change a special kind of externality (p. 24–25).\nQ14.2 State the Ramsey rule and name each parameter. What are the values of δ and η in Stern’s main case?\nQ14.3 What are the two reasons the Review gives for discounting future consumption, and which does it accept?\nQ14.4 What does Stern quote from Ramsey, Pigou, Harrod and Solow? (One phrase each.) Explain\nQ14.5 Why does the SCC depend on the emissions path? Draw the idea of Figure 2.2.\nQ14.6 Explain why a δ of 1% a year is hard to defend if it is read as an extinction hazard.\nQ14.7 Why does “low δ” not imply “low ρ”? Use a numerical example.\nQ14.8 In what sense does one parameter, η, do three jobs?\nQ14.9 Explain Stern’s claim that risk aversion need not be added to the discount rate. Apply\nQ14.10 Compute ρ and the present value of 100 of damage 100 years from now, for (δ, η, g) = (0.1, 1, 2), (1.5, 1.5, 2) and (0.1, 2, 3). Use continuous discounting e−ρt .\nQ14.11 A bank stress test uses a 10-year horizon and a risk-free rate of 7%. The climate scenario you are given values damages using a 1.4% rate. Identify where the two rates differ and what each is for.\nQ14.12 Choose a number for η using Stern’s thought experiment: a poor person gains Rs X and a rich person (twice as rich) loses Rs Y . At what ratio Y /X do you call the change bad? What η does your answer imply? Viva\nQ14.13 “Pure time preference is an empirical fact about how people behave; Stern’s refusal to use it is paternalism.” Respond using both sides of the argument.\nQ14.14 “A thesis on Indian bank capital has no need to take sides on the Stern discount rate.” Agree or disagree, and say where in your thesis the choice would reappear.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA14.1 Global, long-lasting, uncertain, non-marginal (large changes possible).\nA14.2 ρ = δ + ηg. δ = 0.1% and η = 1 (Chapter 6, as quoted in the Postscript)."}, {"pdf": 141, "printed": 114, "ch": 14, "text": "A14.3 Growth (future people are richer) and pure time preference (impatience, chance of nonexistence). It accepts the first, and the second only for the chance of non-existence.\nA14.4 Ramsey: “ethically indefensible”/“weakness of the imagination”. Pigou: “our telescopic faculty is defective”. Harrod: “a polite expression for rapacity and the conquest of reason by passion”. Solow: act as if the social rate of time preference were zero.\nA14.5 More emissions mean higher temperatures; damages are convex, so the marginal damage of a tonne rises with the path. The price and the abatement are chosen together.\nA14.6 e−0.01×100 = 0.368: a 63% chance of the end of the world in a century.\nA14.7 ρ = δ + ηg; with δ = 0, η = 1.5, g = 2.5%: ρ = 3.75%.\nA14.8 Inequality across people at one time, inequality across time (richer descendants), and aversion to risk.\nA14.9 The curvature of utility makes losses in bad states count more; expected utility already embodies that, so a further premium would double-count.\nA14.10 (0.1, 1, 2): ρ = 2.1%, PV = 100e−2.1 = 12.2. (1.5, 1.5, 2): ρ = 4.5%, PV = 1.1. (0.1, 2, 3): ρ = 6.1%, PV = 100e−6.1 = 0.22. The same damage is worth about 11 times more in the first case than in the second, and 55 times more than in the third.\nA14.11 The 7% is a market rate used to value a financial flow over ten years; the 1.4% is a social rate built for consumption over centuries. They answer different questions, and one should not be substituted for the other without saying so.\nA14.12 Your own answer. If, say, you say Y may be up to twice X before you object (the rich person has twice the income), η = 1. If you object only when Y exceeds 4X, η = 2.\nA14.13 For: people do discount; ignoring this overrides preferences. Against (Stern): the question is not private choice but the weight that society places on people who cannot be represented in the decision; and the revealed rates differ widely (they depend on risk, liquidity, and constraints).\nA14.14 Disagree, in part: the thesis can leave the long-run social discount rate aside, but the scenario’s carbon price path, and the choice of horizon in your capital analysis, embody it."}, {"pdf": 142, "printed": 115, "ch": 15, "text": "CHAPTER 15\n\nStern Review: the Postscript and its\nSensitivity Tables\nSource line\nStern Review, “Postscript” (the 2006 postscript, added after the Review’s first publication to answer critics), with its technical annex. In your file it runs from PDF page 617 to PDF page 635 and has no printed page numbers, so I cite it by section and by table number (Tables PA.1 to PA.3), not by page. Your plan lists “Stern Ch.2 + Postscript (2009)”; the file in the folder is the 2006 Postscript, so I use that label throughout. If you meant a different 2009 text, tell me and I will add it.\n\n15.1 What the Postscript is for\nThe Review was published in October 2006 and immediately criticised, mainly by economists who accepted the science but disliked the economics. The Postscript answers three groups of critics and publishes sensitivity tables to show how the headline loss changes when the key assumptions change. Chapter 14 told you what the assumptions are; this chapter shows what they do to the answer. That is the part of the Review a thesis can most usefully borrow: not the number, but the method of exposing the number’s dependence on judgements.\n\n15.2 The headline claims\nClaim\n\nStatement in the Postscript\n\nCost of inaction\n\nBusiness-as-usual damages equal to at least 5% of global consumption\nper head, now and for ever; up to 20% or more when a broader range of\nimpacts and risks is included.\n\nCost of action\n\nAbout 1% of GDP a year by mid-century to stabilise greenhouse gases\nat 500–550 ppm CO2 e, in a range of about plus or minus 3%.\n\nBusiness as usual\n\nConcentrations above 850 ppm CO2 e by the end of the century; at least\na 50% chance of more than 5◦ C of warming.\n\nTable 15.1. The Review’s headline claims as restated in the Postscript. Note that “5%” and “1%” are not comparable on their face: the first is a permanent loss in consumption, expressed as an equivalent constant fall, the second a yearly flow of resource cost."}, {"pdf": 143, "printed": 116, "ch": 15, "text": "15.3 Three critics and Stern’s replies\n1. The science sceptics\nThe Postscript rebuts this group briefly. I did not record the detail of the reply, and since the Review’s science chapters are not in your folder I do not summarise it; see the original if you need it.\n\n2. “Wait and see”\nSome argued that, since the world will be richer, we can act later at lower cost. The Postscript rebuts it. The standard form of the argument, which I give from the logic of the Review and not from the Postscript’s own wording, is that damage depends on the stock of gases, which accumulates; delay raises the stock, and some damage cannot be reversed. It is the non-marginal-path picture of Chapter 14.\n\n3. Pure time preference too low\nHere is the main debate. Critics (the Postscript names Lomborg among them) said that a δ of 0.1% is far below what markets reveal. Stern replies in three steps.\n1. The ethical argument of Chapter 14: no reason to weigh future people less because of when they live.\n2. Even if you disagree, the Tables show that the loss stays large. Raising δ to 1.5% (a high value) reduces the loss but it stays well above 1% of consumption in each case (Tables PA.2, PA.3).\n3. Using an alternative paper by Hope (2006), with δ = 2%, the Postscript reports a social cost of carbon of about $40 per tonne of CO2 , “just over half” of the Review’s central estimate. It is still large compared with the carbon prices then in force.\n\n15.4 The technical annex: the damage function\nThe Review’s Chapter 6 uses the PAGE2002 model (an integrated assessment model, built by Chris Hope, in which uncertain parameters are drawn from probability distributions and the model is run many thousands of times). The damage function is Damage ∝\n\n\u0010 T \u0011γ\n\n2.5\n\nwhere TR is regional warming above the pre-industrial level and γ is the damage exponent. The exponent is uncertain; the baseline treats it as a triangular distribution with minimum 1, mode 1.3 and maximum 3. Meaning: if γ = 1.3, damage rises a little faster than warming; at 5◦ C it is 21.3 ≈ 2.5 times the damage at 2.5◦ C. If γ = 3, the same increase multiplies the damage by 23 = 8. The whole difference between “moderate” and “severe” lies in this exponent, and nobody has data on damages at 5◦ C."}, {"pdf": 144, "printed": 117, "ch": 15, "text": "The sensitivity case raises the exponent: the mode moves to 2.25 and the minimum to 1.5 (maximum unchanged, as far as I read). The Postscript calls this the “high” damage range; I read it as the second set of rows in Tables PA.2 and PA.3, and you should check the table notes against the original. Tail result worth remembering\n\nIn the high-climate scenario, the 95th percentile of the loss reaches 35.2% of GDP per capita by 2200. It shows how much of the Review’s risk sits in a thin upper tail, and links to Weitzman’s argument in Chapter 16. The annex is candid about the limits of such models. It calls integrated assessment models “highly speculative” when used to predict the future with precision, and says the analysis of disaggregated impacts (agriculture, water, health, ecosystems) should come first, with the aggregate model as a way of combining them. In short: the annex advises you not to treat the model output as an observation.\n\n15.5 The sensitivity tables (PA.2 and PA.3)\nThe tables report the equivalent permanent loss of consumption per head (in % a year) for three cases: (a) baseline: market impacts and the risk of catastrophe; (b) adding non-market impacts (health, environment); (c) the high-climate scenario with non-market impacts. “Low range” and “high range” refer to the damage parameter, as above. (a) market + catastrophe (b) + non-market (c) high climate + non-market η = 1.0\n\n5.0\n\n10.9\n\n14.4\n\nη = 1.25\n\n3.8\n\n8.7\n\n12.1\n\nη = 1.5\n\n2.9\n\n6.5\n\n10.2\n\nη = 1.0\n\n6.0\n\n14.2\n\n21.9\n\nHigh range η = 1.25\n\n4.6\n\n11.3\n\n18.2\n\nη = 1.5\n\n3.4\n\n8.7\n\n15.3\n\nLow range\n\nTable 15.2. Table PA.2: equivalent loss of consumption (%) for different values of η, with δ = 0.1%.\n\n(a) market + catastrophe (b) + non-market (c) high climate + non-market\n\nLow range\n\nHigh range\n\nδ = 0.1\n\n5.0\n\n10.9\n\n14.4\n\nδ = 0.5\n\n3.6\n\n8.1\n\n10.6\n\nδ = 1.0\n\n2.3\n\n5.2\n\n6.7\n\nδ = 1.5\n\n1.4\n\n3.3\n\n4.2\n\nδ = 0.1\n\n6.0\n\n14.2\n\n21.9\n\nδ = 0.5\n\n4.3\n\n10.2\n\n15.8\n\nδ = 1.0\n\n2.7\n\n6.4\n\n9.8\n\nδ = 1.5\n\n1.7\n\n4.0\n\n5.9\n\nTable 15.3. Table PA.3: equivalent loss of consumption (%) for different values of δ, with η = 1."}, {"pdf": 145, "printed": 118, "ch": 15, "text": "Equivalent loss of consumption (%)\n\nLow damage range\n\nHigh damage range\nmarket + catastrophe\n+ non-market\n\nhigh climate + non-market\n\n0.1\n0.5\n1.0\n1.5\nPure time preference δ (% a year), η = 1\n\n0.1\n0.5\n1.0\n1.5\nPure time preference δ (% a year), η = 1\n\nFigure 15.1. The loss in Table PA.3 plotted against δ (my chart from the table; the dotted line is 1%, the order of magnitude of the cost of action). Every bar remains above the dotted line.\n\nHow to read the tables: four lessons\n1. δ matters a lot, but not enough to reverse the conclusion. Moving from δ = 0.1 to 1.5 keeps only 27–30% of the loss in every case (for example 3.3/10.9 = 0.30 and 4.2/14.4 = 0.29). Even the smallest entry in Table PA.3 (1.4%) exceeds the 1% cost of action.\n2. η matters about as much. In the low range, case (b), going from η = 1 to 1.5 takes the loss from 10.9 to 6.5, a 40% cut. Higher inequality aversion lowers the weight on richer future people, so the loss is smaller.\n3. Including non-market impacts roughly doubles the loss (column (a) to column (b): 5.0 to 10.9 and 6.0 to 14.2).\n4. The damage range matters most when the climate outcome is bad: at δ = 0.1,\nη = 1, the gap between low and high range is 1.0 point in column (a) (5.0 against 6.0) but 7.5 points in column (c) (14.4 against 21.9). A caution about the comparison with 1% Comparing “a permanent loss of 1.4% of consumption” with “a cost of 1% of GDP a year” hides two things. First, the cost of action is also uncertain (the plus or minus 3%). Second, the two figures are built on different bases. Use the comparison as the Postscript does, as an order-of-magnitude argument, and not as a cost-benefit ratio.\n\n15.6 Common misreadings\nFour things people get wrong\n\n1. “Stern’s 5% is the loss in 2100.” No: it is a constant-equivalent loss spread across all future dates and all states of the world, including the catastrophic ones.\n2. “The 20% is a forecast.” It is the value when more impacts and risks are included, in a particular parameter setting."}, {"pdf": 146, "printed": 119, "ch": 15, "text": "3. “High discount rates refute Stern.” The tables show the loss falls but stays above the cost of action.\n4. “The annex proves the model is right.” The annex says the opposite: it describes the model as highly speculative and puts the disaggregated analysis first.\n\n15.7 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference, not the Review’s.\n• A template for a sensitivity section. Tables PA.2–PA.3 are a good template for a thesis chapter that varies two or three assumptions one at a time and reports the effect on the headline result. If your thesis simulates capital ratios under scenarios, a table of the same kind (rows: the scenario’s carbon price path and the loss given default; columns: the capital ratio at different dates) would serve.\n• Be wary of quoting the numbers as findings. They are the output of one model (PAGE2002) with one set of distributions. Quote them as “Stern Review’s estimates”, not as facts.\n• Chain to Phase 5–6. The 95th-percentile result is the climate version of the tail-risk idea. Banks care about tails: expected shortfall and CRISK measure the tail of the loss distribution, not its mean. Do you need the original? For the headline claims, the critics and replies, and the tables: no. Everything above is in your notes; the table entries are copied from Tables PA.2–PA.3. Check the original if you quote: the exact definition of “low range” and “high range”, the wording on Hope (2006), and the statement of the 95th-percentile figure. I read the tables and the annex text in the file but not every footnote. Skip: the long list of references.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ15.1 State the Postscript’s three headline claims (cost of inaction, cost of action, businessas-usual outcome).\nQ15.2 What are the three groups of critics, and what is the one-line reply to each?\nQ15.3 Write the damage function of the annex and name its parameters.\nQ15.4 What does the Postscript say about the status of integrated assessment models? Explain\nQ15.5 Why do the “5%” and the “1%” not compare directly?\nQ15.6 Why does a higher η lower the loss in Table PA.2?"}, {"pdf": 147, "printed": 120, "ch": 15, "text": "Q15.7 What does the Hope (2006) reference show, and why does Stern cite it?\nQ15.8 Why does the damage exponent γ matter more than any data we have?\nApply\nQ15.9 Using Table PA.3, compute the ratio of the loss at δ = 1.5 to the loss at δ = 0.1 for each of the six cases (low and high range, columns a, b, c). What is the smallest ratio and what does it say?\nQ15.10 For TR = 2.5 and TR = 5, compute (TR /2.5)γ for γ = 1, 1.3, 2.25, 3. Which γ would you defend and why?\nQ15.11 Design a one-page sensitivity table for your own thesis, in the style of PA.3: name the two parameters, the three or four values, and the outcome in each cell. Viva\nQ15.12 “The Postscript is just a defence; the real finding is the original Review.” Reply.\nQ15.13 “If the loss stays above 1% for every δ, the choice of δ is irrelevant.” Evaluate.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA15.1 At least 5% (up to 20%) of consumption lost for ever; about 1% of GDP (plus or minus 3) to stabilise at 500–550 ppm CO2 e; above 850 ppm and a 50% chance of more than 5◦ C.\nA15.2 Science sceptics (the review follows the literature); wait and see (stock accumulates and some damage is irreversible); too low a δ (ethical argument and tables).\nA15.3 Damage ∝ (TR /2.5)γ ; TR regional warming; γ triangular(1, 1.3, 3) in the baseline.\nA15.4 That they are “highly speculative” and that the analysis of disaggregated impacts should come first.\nA15.5 The first is a constant-equivalent loss in consumption from all future dates; the second is a yearly flow cost; the 1% also has a range of about 3 points either side.\nA15.6 With higher η, a given loss falls on richer people (the future is richer) and counts for less.\nA15.7 With δ = 2% the SCC is still about $40, just over half the central estimate: even a high δ leaves a high value.\nA15.8 Because no observations exist at high temperatures; the choice between 1.3 and 3 changes the result by a factor of several and is not testable.\nA15.9 Low range: a 0.28, b 0.30, c 0.29. High range: a 0.28, b 0.28, c 0.27 (1.7/6.0, 4.0/14.2, 5.9/21.9). The smallest is about 0.27: raising δ fifteen-fold removes only about 70–73% of the loss.\nA15.10 TR = 5 gives 2γ : 2, 2.46, 4.76, 8; TR = 2.5 gives 1 in every case. A defence of any value must rest on the physical and economic literature on impacts, not on the model.\nA15.11 Example: rows δ and η, cells the estimated fall in the capital adequacy ratio. Any"}, {"pdf": 148, "printed": 121, "ch": 15, "text": "sensible answer names the scenario, the parameter values, and the unit.\nA15.12 The Postscript adds sensitivity tables and answers the critics; it does not change the model. A viva answer should say what it adds (the robustness) and what it does not (a new estimate).\nA15.13 Not irrelevant: the size of the loss varies by a factor of three or more (14.4 down to 4.2). It is irrelevant only to the sign of the policy conclusion; for the size of the carbon price the choice matters a great deal."}, {"pdf": 149, "printed": 122, "ch": 16, "text": "CHAPTER 16\n\nWeitzman (2009): On Modeling and\nInterpreting the Economics of Catastrophic Climate Change\nSource line\nMartin L. Weitzman, “On Modeling and Interpreting the Economics of Catastrophic Climate Change”, Review of Economics and Statistics 91(1), February 2009, pp. 1–19. Page numbers are the journal’s printed pages, which equal the PDF pages of the file I read (it came from your Layer 2 folder, not the coursework folder). Everything in this chapter comes from the full text, Sections I to VIII. The article is a mathematical-statistical argument with a short numerical illustration and a long, candid discussion of what it means.\n\n16.1 The question in one paragraph\nWeitzman’s starting point (p. 1) is that the essence of the climate problem is not only discounting, and not only ordinary risk: it is deep structural uncertainty, “the unknowns”, together with an inability to put a meaningful value on losses from disastrous warming. He proves that when such uncertainty is combined with unlimited exposure to loss and relative risk aversion above one, the usual expected-value cost-benefit calculation can be dominated by the very worst outcomes, however improbable. He calls this the Dismal Theorem (p. 10). Whatever you believe about climate, the argument is a general result about how tail risk should be handled, and so it speaks directly to a thesis about bank capital.\n\n16.2 The empirical motivation (Sections I–II, pp. 1–6)\nWeitzman argues that if one is forced to give a “best guess” of the extreme bad tail of the distribution of long-run warming under only gradually ramped-up remedies, then global mean warming in about two centuries will be greater than 10◦ C with a probability of “somewhere around 0.05”, and greater than 20◦ C with “somewhere around 0.01” (p. 2). A world 10–20◦ C hotter has been unknown for hundreds of millions of years, and the speed of change might be unprecedented “even on a timescale of billions of years”. Whatever one thinks of the numbers, the argument is that the probabilities are not remotely negligible."}, {"pdf": 150, "printed": 123, "ch": 16, "text": "Climate sensitivity as the amplifier\nThe key quantity is climate sensitivity: the long-run warming that follows a doubling of CO2 . Weitzman uses it as an example of a scaling-up factor with an uncertain value.\n• Based on 22 studies summarised by IPCC-AR4, the probability that S1 exceeds 7◦ C is about 5%, and that it exceeds 10◦ C about 1%.\n• A feedback view: S1 = 1.2/(1 − g1 ), where 1.2◦ C is the no-feedback response and g1 is the total feedback gain. Because the gain appears in a denominator, a small uncertainty in g1 becomes a large uncertainty in S1 when g1 is near 1.\n• Adding one feedback, the release of carbon from thawing permafrost (Torn and Harte), raises the gain by 0.067 (g2 = g1 + 0.067, S2 = 1.2/(1 − g2 )). Then the 5% and 1% thresholds move to 11.5◦ C and 22.6◦ C. The lesson: the tail of the output is much fatter than the tail of the inputs, because of the nonlinear amplification, and adding a feedback that is only partly known fattens it again.\n\n16.3 The model (Sections III–IV, pp. 6–10)\nSetup\nThere are two periods. Welfare uses constant relative risk aversion (CRRA) utility: U (C) = C 1−η /(1 − η), with η the coefficient of relative risk aversion (the same η as in Chapter 14). Let Y = ln C, the log of future consumption relative to today. The stochastic discount factor is M = βe−ηY , where β = e−δ . The price of a unit of future consumption is its expectation, E[M ] = β e−ηy f (y) dy, which is the moment-generating function of the distribution of Y evaluated at −η. Why the moment-generating function matters Bad outcomes are low y (consumption falls). The factor e−ηy is huge when y is very negative: a disaster is weighted exponentially. Whether the sum e−ηy f (y)dy is small or infinite depends on whether f (y) goes to zero in the bad tail faster than e−ηy grows. Thin tails win that race; fat tails lose it.\n\nBenchmark: the normal case\nIf Y is normal with mean µ and standard deviation s, then E[M ] = exp(−δ − ηµ + 12 η 2 s2 ), and the risk-free rate is rf = δ + ηµ − 21 η 2 s2 . This is Ramsey’s rule (Chapter 14) with an added precautionary term − 12 η 2 s2 : more variance lowers the rate, because people want to save more against bad outcomes. The correction is finite, and it is the same kind of term you would find in any finance text.\n\nThe step that changes everything: the scale is unknown\nSuppose the scale s is itself unknown, and all that is known is a few data points. Weitzman uses a scale-invariant prior, p0 (s) ∝ s−k , and n observations. The resulting posterior-predictive"}, {"pdf": 151, "printed": 124, "ch": 16, "text": "distribution of Y is a Student-t distribution (equation 12), whose tails fall only as a power law with exponent n + k. This is a fat tail. For a fat-tailed f , the integral for E[M ] diverges: E[M ] = +∞ (equation 13). The result traces back to Geweke (2001), who noted the limits of CRRA utility. Intuition: even after you learn from n data points, you remain unsure how wide the true distribution is. Averaging over all possible widths gives a mixture, and the wide ones dominate the extreme tail.\n\n10−5\n10−7\n10−9\n10−11\n10−13\n10−15\n\nWhat the discount factor adds up (η = 2)\n7.5\n\nNormal (thin tail)\n\nlog10 of e^(−ηy) × density\n\nProbability of exceeding it\n\nSame variance, very different tails\n\nStudent-t, 4 d.f. (fat tail)\n\nSize of bad outcome (standard deviations)\n\nNormal\nStudent-t, 4 d.f.\n\n5.0\n2.5\n0.0\n−2.5\n−5.0\n−7.5\n−10.0\n\n−10\ny = log of consumption growth (bad on the left)\n\nFigure 16.1. Left: the chance of exceeding a bad outcome of a given size in standard-deviation units, for a normal and for a Student-t with 4 degrees of freedom scaled to have the same variance (my chart). Right: the integrand of the expected discount factor, e−ηy f (y), on a log scale, with η = 2. For the normal it falls away on the left; for the t it keeps rising as y → −∞, so the area under it has no limit.\n\nA numerical feel (my arithmetic)\nTable 16.1 compares the two distributions with equal variance. At 5 standard deviations the normal gives 3 in ten million; the t gives about 1 in a thousand, nearly 4,000 times larger. At 8 standard deviations the normal gives 6 × 10−16 and the t about 1.7 in ten thousand. Two distributions can have the same mean and variance and yet disagree absurdly about the disaster. Size of bad outcome (std. dev.)\n\nNormal\n\nStudent-t, 4 d.f.\n\n0.023\n\n0.024\n\n0.0014\n\n0.0011\n\n−16\n\n0.00017\n\n5 2.9 × 10\n8 6.2 × 10\n\n0.0066\n\nTable 16.1. One-sided tail probabilities, same variance (computed by me with standard routines).\n\nClosing the model: the consumption floor and the value of statistical civilization An infinite E[M ] is not a sensible answer; it says people would pay everything to avoid even a tiny risk. Weitzman therefore adds a floor D(λ) below which consumption cannot fall (or, equivalently, the point at which the disutility is capped). The floor is tied to a “VSL-like"}, {"pdf": 152, "printed": 125, "ch": 16, "text": "parameter” λ, the value of a statistical life (or, much more broadly, “statistical civilization”) measured in units of per-capita consumption (equation 16): \u0003−1/(η−1) D(λ) = 1 + (η − 1)λ η > 1. With η ≈ 2 and λ ≈ 100 (about what the value-of-statistical-life literature suggests, p. 10 footnote 9) one obtains D ≈ 0.01 (consumption can fall to 1% of normal). With λ ≈ 1000, D ≈ 0.001. The value of λ for “all of civilization” is presumably much higher than 100, and Weitzman admits that at best one might get within an order of magnitude. Theorem 1: the Dismal Theorem (p. 10) Let E[M |λ] be the expected discount factor with the floor D(λ). For any n and k, lim E[M |λ] = +∞.\n\nλ→∞\n\nIn words: the more you value the avoidance of total catastrophe, the more the expected value of the discount factor grows without limit, whatever the amount of data and whatever the other parameters. Weitzman describes the underlying mechanism as nonuniform convergence: for each fixed floor, the answer is finite, but how fast it converges depends on the floor, so no single cutoff gives a reliable approximation.\n\nNormal: levels off at e² ≈ 7.4\n\nTruncated E[M] (η = 2)\n\n1010\n\nStudent-t: no limit\n\n108\n106\n104\n102\n100\n2.5\n5.0\n7.5\n10.0 12.5 15.0 17.5 20.0\nLowest outcome allowed in the sum, L (log points of consumption lost)\n\nFigure 16.2. Nonuniform convergence made visible (my computation, η = 2, scale 1, 4 d.f.). The vertical axis is the expected discount factor when outcomes worse than L log-points are excluded. For the normal it levels off at e2 ≈ 7.4 by L ≈ 6; for the t it keeps growing: 53 at L = 6, 9,400 at L = 10, about 1011 at L = 20. A cost-benefit analyst who cuts the tail at some “reasonable” L is choosing the answer.\n\nA worked example: what would you pay? (p. 15; my reconstruction)\nTake η = 2, so U (C) = −1/C. Suppose there is a 0.5% chance that consumption falls to D = 0.001 of today’s level (λ ≈ 1000). Without insurance, expected utility is 0.995 × (−1) +\n0.005 × (−1000) = −5.995. A sure payment of a fraction w of consumption gives −1/(1 − w). Setting −1/(1 − w) = −5.995 gives 1 − w = 0.167, so w ≈ 0.83. The willingness to pay is about 83% of consumption, which is the figure Weitzman quotes for this case (p. 15). Compare the expected loss of consumption: 0.005 × 0.999 ≈ 0.5%. The two figures differ by a factor of about 170. That gap is the catastrophe-insurance aspect dominating the pure-risk aspect."}, {"pdf": 153, "printed": 126, "ch": 16, "text": "16.4 What it is not (Section VI, pp. 12–15)\nWeitzman anticipates the objection that this is an impossibility theorem or that it would freeze all progress. His reply has several parts.\n• Not an impossibility result. The theorem does not say that nothing can be computed; it says that the answer is sensitive to what is assumed about the extreme tail and about λ, and that sensitivity cannot be removed by collecting more data of the ordinary kind. Where the dismal theorem applies, there is no assurance that a finite cutoff is a good approximation (p. 13).\n• A “rule of reason”. The relevance of the “generalized precautionary principle” in a given application depends on how prior knowledge of λ and k combines with the information in the n observations to fatten or to thin the tail; it is to be judged case by case (p. 13).\n• Comparisons. Other fat-tailed risks (GMOs, asteroid impact) are judged by the same logic; Weitzman argues that a climate cataclysm is on the order of 10,000 times more probable than an asteroid impact of the 1-in-100-million-year kind (my reading of pp. 13– 14; check against the original).\n• Direction, not magnitude. The qualitative advice is “relatively more cautious” about emissions; he admits that how much more is not known. He also notes that the bad tails of the solutions (nuclear power, geoengineering, ocean sequestration) must be compared.\n• A rejection of the de minimis cut. Setting small, imprecise probabilities “precisely to 0” is “arbitrary and problematic” (p. 13).\n\n16.5 Damage functions and what to do in practice (Section VII,\npp. 15–18)\n100\n\nwhere data\nexist\n\nLoss of output (%)\n\nQuadratic 1/(1+γT²)\nExponential exp(−γT²)\nConvex, 50% loss at 6°C\n\nWarming (°C)\n\nFigure 16.3. Three damage functions that agree where data exist (up to about 3◦ C) and disagree wildly beyond it. The first two are calibrated to a 2% loss at 3◦ C (my illustrative calibration); the third is calibrated to 2% at 3◦ C and 50% at 6◦ C and gives a loss of 99% at 12◦ C. The figure shows the point, and is not an estimate."}, {"pdf": 154, "printed": 127, "ch": 16, "text": "Weitzman’s practical critique of existing integrated assessment models is that their damage functions are, in his words, quadratic in warming, of the form 1/[1+γ(∆T )2 ], with no compelling rationale for the exponent (Section VII, p. 15–16). He prefers a form such as exp(−γ(∆T )2 ), which falls faster as warming increases, and, in footnote 17, notes that the Stern Review base case has δ ≈ 0 and η ≈ 1 (I did not record what he concludes from this). He makes three practical recommendations.\n1. Be open about the tail assumptions. The damage function at high temperatures is a subjective guess; say so.\n2. Oversample the extreme tail in Monte Carlo runs. The range of the grid and the number of runs have to grow together, or the simulations will not reach the region that matters.\n3. Fund research into geoengineering as a form of insurance against the worst, and think of climate policy as catastrophe insurance, noting (footnote 21) that the United States spends something like 3% of national income on a clean environment.\n\n16.6 The conclusion (Section VIII, pp. 18–19)\nThe last section is as important as the proof. Weitzman says plainly that the theorem “makes economic analysis trickier and more open-ended” and that the analysis “appears less scientifically conclusive and more contentiously subjective” than ordinary CBA. His advice to the climate economist is not to present a cost-benefit estimate for an inherently fat-tailed situation “as if it is accurate and objective”, and not even to present it as an approximation to something accurate and objective; instead the economist should stress that the estimate “might conceivably be arbitrarily inaccurate” depending on what is assumed about the high-temperature damage function and the fatness of the tails. He warns of the “artificial crispness” of conventional IAM-based CBA, and of “misplaced concreteness”. The contribution of the paper, he says, is to state a principle: under positive relative risk aversion and potentially unlimited exposure, the catastrophe-insurance aspect can dominate the social-discounting aspect, the pure-risk aspect and the consumption-smoothing aspect.\n\n16.7 Common misreadings\nFive things people get wrong\n\n1. “The theorem says expected loss is infinite, so we should spend everything.” No: it says the unbounded model gives an infinite answer, so the answer depends on the floor and on the VSL-like parameter. Weitzman explicitly says it is not an impossibility theorem and requires a rule of reason (pp. 12–13).\n2. “It needs an ad hoc ambiguity aversion.” The paper’s stress is that the result comes from ordinary expected utility with Bayesian learning, plus structural uncertainty; no special postulate is needed. (Stern’s Chapter 2, by contrast, points to the ambiguity-aversion literature as a separate branch.)\n3. “The 83% is a forecast of what we should pay.” It is a stylised calculation"}, {"pdf": 155, "printed": 128, "ch": 16, "text": "Stern (Chapters 14–15)\n\nWeitzman (2009)\n\nMain lever\n\nDiscounting (δ, η)\n\nThe shape of the tail (n, k, λ)\n\nTreatment of risk\n\nExpected utility; mean of a distribution The mean may not exist; what matters\ncomputed by simulation\nis the tail\n\nRole of η\n\nInequality and risk aversion; the loss Must exceed 1 for the floor to make\nfalls as η rises (Table PA.2)\nsense; the effect of η on the tail is the\nopposite (a higher η raises the weight\non disaster)\n\nPolicy message\n\nStrong early action is warranted even at Caution warranted, size\nhigher δ\npresent analysis honestly\n\nAttitude to IAMs\n\nHighly speculative; start from disaggre- Artificial crispness; misplaced concretegated impacts ness\n\nunknown;\n\nTable 16.2. Stern and Weitzman side by side. The last-but-one row on η is partly my inference: Weitzman needs η > 1 for his floor, and in e−ηy a larger η magnifies the weight of bad outcomes.\n\nwith λ = 1000 and a probability of 0.005.\n4. “Weitzman disagrees with Stern on the discount rate.” Weitzman agrees\nthat the discount rate matters; his claim is that the tail can matter more than the rate.\n5. “The temperatures (10–20◦ C) are predictions.” They are an illustration of a “best guess” for the bad tail.\n\n16.8 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference, not Weitzman’s.\n• A theoretical case for tail measures. Weitzman supplies a reason for a bank supervisor, or your thesis, to look at tail measures (expected shortfall, Conditional Value at Risk, CRISK in Phase 6) in preference to a mean loss: if the tail is fat, the mean tells you little. You may cite the theory without adopting his numbers.\n• The Knight–Keynes link. Weitzman’s “structural uncertainty” is the technical form of the uncertainty that Phase 1 distinguishes from risk: you do not know the distribution’s scale, and your own data cannot settle it.\n• A caution about capital. The extra capital that would insure against a fat tail has no finite “right” answer; the regulator picks a confidence level (such as 99.9%), and Weitzman’s point is that for a fat-tailed distribution the answer is much more sensitive to that choice than the usual models suggest. Treat this as a motivation for a robustness check, not as a result you can test with bank data. Do you need the original? For the argument, the intuition and the examples: no. I have given the model, the theorem, the worked example (checked by hand: 83%), and the practical lessons. Yes, if you plan to present the proof: read pp. 6–10; I describe the steps without"}, {"pdf": 156, "printed": 129, "ch": 16, "text": "reproducing the algebra of equations (3)–(16).\nCheck against the original before quoting: the comparison with asteroid impact (pp. 13–14), the 3% figure in footnote 21, and the page numbers of the damage-function discussion; I have given them from my reading of the text, and some are paraphrased. Not needed: the reference list.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ16.1 What are Weitzman’s two ballpark figures for the bad tail of long-run warming (p. 2)?\nQ16.2 Write the stochastic discount factor and the expected discount factor for normal Y . Which term is the precautionary term?\nQ16.3 State Theorem 1. What are n, k, λ?\nQ16.4 What does the consumption floor D(λ) equal for η = 2, λ = 100 and 1000?\nExplain\nQ16.5 Why does an unknown scale produce a fat-tailed posterior-predictive distribution?\nQ16.6 Explain nonuniform convergence with Figure 16.2.\nQ16.7 Why is a damage function that is accurate up to 3◦ C of little help in the argument?\nQ16.8 Why is the Dismal Theorem not an impossibility result?\nQ16.9 Contrast the role of η in Stern and in Weitzman.\nApply\nQ16.10 Redo the willingness-to-pay example with η = 2, a catastrophe probability of 1% and a floor D = 0.01. What fraction of consumption would you pay? Compare it with the expected loss.\nQ16.11 Using Table 16.1, how many times more likely is a 5-standard-deviation loss under the t than under the normal? What does that imply for a bank that sets capital with a normal model?\nQ16.12 You must write one paragraph for a regulator summarising what Weitzman implies for a climate stress test. Draft it. Viva\nQ16.13 “Weitzman shows that cost-benefit analysis is impossible for climate; so use the precautionary principle.” Evaluate.\nQ16.14 “Fat-tailed climate risk is no different from the fat tails already in credit portfolios.” Agree or disagree."}, {"pdf": 157, "printed": 130, "ch": 16, "text": "Answer sketches\nA16.1 Probability around 0.05 of warming above 10◦ C and around 0.01 of warming above 20◦ C in about two centuries.\nA16.2 M = βe−ηY ; for normal Y , E[M ] = exp(−δ − ηµ + 12 η 2 s2 ), and the term in s2 is the precautionary term.\nA16.3 For any n, k: limλ→∞ E[M |λ] = +∞. n is the number of observations, k the exponent of the prior on the scale, λ the VSL-like parameter.\nA16.4 0.01 and 0.001, from D = [1 + (η − 1)λ]−1/(η−1) .\nA16.5 Averaging over the possible scales puts weight on very wide distributions, whose tails dominate; the mixture is a Student-t with a power-law tail. 2 2\n\nA16.6 For the normal the curve flattens at eη s /2 = 7.4; for the t it keeps rising, so the result depends on where one cuts the tail.\nA16.7 Because the theorem concerns the region beyond the data, which the function does not constrain; see Figure 16.3.\nA16.8 It says that the model with unbounded exposure gives no finite answer; the answer depends on λ and the tail, so judgement is needed (the rule of reason).\nA16.9 Stern: η governs inequality and risk aversion, and a higher η lowers the loss because the future is richer. Weitzman: η > 1 is needed for the floor, and a higher η raises the weight on disaster.\nA16.10 D = 0.01, so U (D) = −100. Expected utility = 0.99(−1) + 0.01(−100) = −1.99. 1 − w = 1/1.99 = 0.50: w ≈ 50%. Expected loss = 0.01 × 0.99 ≈ 1%. The gap is a factor of about 50.\nA16.11 1.06 × 10−3 /2.87 × 10−7 ≈ 3,700. The bank would hold far too little capital against an event it regards as nearly impossible.\nA16.12 A sound answer: use scenarios and tail measures, state the assumed tail openly, run sensitivity on the floor/cutoff, and do not present a single capital number as if it were precise.\nA16.13 The Dismal Theorem implies that a finite cutoff is not guaranteed to give a good approximation, so cost-benefit numbers should be presented with their dependence on assumptions; it does not say CBA is impossible or that the precautionary principle mechanically decides.\nA16.14 Partly agree: both have fat tails and the statistics are similar. Disagree on what can be learned: a bank has data on many loans and years (the n is large and the structure repeated), while climate has one planet; the learning that would thin the tail is not available."}, {"pdf": 158, "printed": 131, "ch": 17, "text": "CHAPTER 17\n\nDietz and Stern (2015): Endogenous\nGrowth, Convexity of Damages and Climate Risk\nSource line\nSimon Dietz and Nicholas Stern, “Endogenous growth, convexity of damage and climate risk: how Nordhaus’ framework supports deep cuts in carbon emissions”, Economic Journal 125(583), 2015, pp. 574–620 (DOI 10.1111/ecoj.12188; open copy at LSE eprints, eprints.lse.ac.uk/58406). Read this first: how this chapter was made This paper is not in your folder, and I have not read it. I could fetch only a web summary of it, which gave the structure of the model, the main numerical results, and the conclusions, but not the equations or the tables. Everything below is therefore at the level of what the paper does and finds, and every number is marked “check against the original” before you cite it. The summary’s page header (“547–620”) disagrees with the journal range I give above (574–620); I use 574–620, which is the one consistent with the volume, but verify it. If you download the paper and put it in the folder, I will replace this chapter with one checked line by line.\n\n17.1 What the paper is trying to do\nWilliam Nordhaus’s DICE model (Dynamic Integrated model of Climate and the Economy; the original is Nordhaus 1991, “To slow or not to slow”) is the most widely used integrated assessment model in economics. It is a growth model in which an economy chooses saving and a rate of emissions control, emissions raise temperature, and temperature reduces output. Nordhaus’s results have been used to argue for modest early cuts with larger cuts later, a “climate policy ramp”. Dietz and Stern keep Nordhaus’s framework, his social parameters, and his calibration, and change three things that they consider unrealistic. They ask: if you only change the model structure, and leave the ethics alone, do you still get modest cuts? Their answer is no. The design, in one line Same ethics, better structure. Dietz and Stern leave the social parameters at DICE values (pure time preference 1.5% a year, η = 1.5; both from the summary I read, check) and change the economics and risk, not the ethics. Therefore their argument is not the"}, {"pdf": 159, "printed": 132, "ch": 17, "text": "Stern–Nordhaus one about δ (Chapter 15); it works on the other three levers in Figure 18.1.\n\n17.2 The three changes\n1. Endogenous growth\nIn DICE, the growth of the economy is given from outside (technology improves at an assumed rate); climate damage lowers the level of output but does not alter the growth rate. Dietz and Stern let the growth rate depend on investment and on the stock of knowledge. They build two versions, as I read the summary.\n• Model 1.K: knowledge is proportional to capital (an “AK”-type specification), so investment lifts both output and knowledge.\n• Model 1.TFP: total factor productivity itself is endogenous.\nWhy it matters: if climate damage reduces investment, it reduces future productivity as well, so damages compound. A fall in the growth rate is much more costly than a one-time fall in the level, a point you can check with egt . Damage in an endogenous growth model is therefore a larger, longer-lasting loss than in DICE.\n\n2. Convex damages\nDICE uses a quadratic damage function with modest losses at high temperatures. Dietz and Stern replace it with a more convex function, using a modification proposed by Weitzman (2012), calibrated so that warming of 6◦ C costs 50% of output, together with a “high damage” case with a 50% loss at 4◦ C (from the summary, check). Chapter 16 and Figure 16.3 show why the choice matters: data cannot discriminate between functions beyond about 3◦ C.\n\n3. Climate risk\nDICE uses a single value for climate sensitivity. Dietz and Stern let sensitivity S be stochastic, with a log-logistic distribution with mean of about 2.9◦ C and a 95th percentile of about 5.3◦ C (a discrete version uses S ∈ {1.5, 3, 6}; check). The model is solved under this uncertainty and the planner maximises expected utility. This is the finite, structured version of the risk that Weitzman’s paper warns about: it includes a fat-ish right tail but with a bounded, specified distribution.\n\n17.3 Results (all “check against original”)\nThe message, as the paper’s title says: “how Nordhaus’ framework supports deep cuts in carbon emissions”. The title is the thesis. The results are the same type of recommendation as Stern’s, but now arrived at without changing the discounting that Nordhaus had used."}, {"pdf": 160, "printed": 133, "ch": 17, "text": "800\n0.4\n\n700\nPeak CO₂ (ppm)\n\nOptimal 2015 emissions control rate\n\nChapter 17. Dietz and Stern (2015)\n\n0.3\n\n0.2\n\n0.1\n\n600\n500\n400\n300\n200\n100\n\n0.0\n\nStandard DICE\n\nCapital models\n(range)\n\nStandard DICE\n\nTFP models\n(range)\n\nDietz–Stern\n(range)\n\nFigure 17.1. Headline results as reported in the summary I read (check each against the paper). Left: the optimal emissions control rate in 2015 (the fraction of emissions cut): 0.158 in standard DICE; 0.213–0.342 in the capital-based models; 0.272–0.396 in the TFP models. Right: the peak concentration of CO2 (ppm): about 735 in standard DICE against 444–524 in the new models. Ranges are across the model variants. Quantity\n\nReported value\n\nOptimal 2015 control rate\n\n0.158 (standard DICE); 0.213–0.342 (capital models); 0.272–0.396\n(TFP models)\n\n2015 carbon price\n\nabout $44 per tonne of carbon (DICE); up to about $233 per\ntonne of carbon in the new models\n\nPeak CO2\n\nabout 735 ppm (DICE); 444–524 ppm (new models)\n\nSuggested 2015 price range\n\n$32–103 per tonne of CO2 (2012 prices), rising to $82–260 within\ntwo decades\n\nTable 17.1. The paper’s main numbers as given in the summary. A price “per tonne of carbon” is\n3.67 times a price “per tonne of CO2 ”, because a tonne of carbon corresponds to 44/12 tonnes of CO2 ; so $44 per tonne of carbon is about $12 per tonne of CO2 , and $233 per tonne of carbon about $63.\n\n17.4 Limits stated or implied\n• The summary says the model has no adjustment costs: capital and abatement can be changed freely. Realistically, rapid transition would be more costly, which would weaken the case for rapid cuts.\n• The results rest on the calibration of the convex damage function and of the endogenous growth parameters, neither of which is observed.\n• The climate-risk treatment is a specified distribution, not the deep structural uncertainty that Weitzman describes (Chapter 16)."}, {"pdf": 161, "printed": 134, "ch": 17, "text": "17.5 Common misreadings\nThree things to avoid\n\n1. “Dietz and Stern lower the discount rate.” No: they keep Nordhaus’s.\n2. “Their carbon price is the Stern price.” It is the output of a different model with different results; compare ranges, not point values.\n3. “The paper tests the model against data.” It is a calibrated simulation study.\n\n17.6 What it means for the thesis\nMy inference, not the paper’s.\n• Calibration of shocks. Phase 6 will need GDP-shock parameters for the transition scenario. This paper is a source of plausible ranges for carbon prices (2015 prices and rises over two decades), but it is a model of the world economy and not of Indian banks. Cite it for what the carbon price should be, not for how a bank is hit.\n• The model structure matters for losses. If growth is endogenous, the impact on productivity (and therefore on borrowers’ ability to repay) is long-lasting, which supports a reading of transition risk as persistent and not transient.\n• Linking carbon prices to the NGFS scenarios. The NGFS scenarios (Phase 6) give carbon price paths; this paper helps judge whether those are high or low relative to what economic models say. Do you need the original? Yes, if you intend to cite any number from this chapter. I read only a summary. Download the paper (LSE eprints, link above); the key places to check are the model description (Section 2), the damage-function calibration, the table of optimal control rates and carbon prices, and the discussion of caveats. No, if you only need the idea: that Nordhaus’s own framework yields larger early cuts once growth is endogenous, damages are convex and climate sensitivity is uncertain.\n\nCheck yourself\nRecall\nQ17.1 What are the three modifications Dietz and Stern make to DICE?\nQ17.2 Which parameters of DICE do they leave unchanged?\nQ17.3 State the optimal 2015 control rates reported for standard DICE and for the two families of new models. (Check the numbers.) Explain\nQ17.4 Why does endogenous growth make climate damage more costly than in DICE?"}, {"pdf": 162, "printed": 135, "ch": 17, "text": "Q17.5 Why do the authors emphasise that they leave the discounting at DICE values?\nQ17.6 Explain how a stochastic climate sensitivity raises the optimal cut even if the mean sensitivity is unchanged.\nQ17.7 What does the absence of adjustment costs imply for the interpretation of the results? Apply\nQ17.8 Convert $44 and $233 per tonne of carbon to per tonne of CO2 .\nQ17.9 Model A: a one-off 5% fall in the level of output. Model B: growth is 0.5 points lower (1.5% instead of 2%) for 10 years. Compare the output gaps after 10 years. Then compare them if B’s lower growth lasts 50 years. Viva\nQ17.10 “A model that reaches deep cuts by changing only the structure gives more credible support than one that reaches them by changing the ethics.” Discuss.\nQ17.11 “Because the paper is a calibrated simulation, it says nothing about Indian banks.” How would you use it responsibly?\n\nAnswer sketches\nA17.1 Endogenous growth (capital and TFP versions), convex damages (calibrated to 50% at 6◦ C, with a high case at 4◦ C), and stochastic climate sensitivity.\nA17.2 The social parameters: pure time preference 1.5% and η = 1.5 (check).\nA17.3 0.158; 0.213–0.342; 0.272–0.396.\nA17.4 Damage reduces investment and so the growth of knowledge and productivity; the loss compounds.\nA17.5 So that the result cannot be attributed to the ethical choice that divides Stern and Nordhaus.\nA17.6 Because of the convexity of damage, the high-sensitivity states cost more than the low-sensitivity states save; the expected marginal damage rises.\nA17.7 Smooth adjustment is assumed; rapid cuts could cost more, so the results may overstate the case for a fast start.\nA17.8 About $12 and $63 per tonne of CO2 (44/3.67 and 233/3.67).\nA17.9 Model A: output is 5% below baseline for ever. Model B after 10 years: (1.015/1.02)10 = 0.952, a gap of about 4.8%, so close to A. If the slower growth lasts 50 years the gap is 1 − (1.015/1.02)50 ≈ 22%. Lesson: a growth effect is far worse than a level effect if it persists.\nA17.10 A balanced answer: structural changes can be debated on evidence, whereas ethics cannot be tested; but the structural changes are also calibrated judgements (the convexity of damage), so credibility is a matter of degree.\nA17.11 Use it for the price range of carbon and for the direction of the effect; then build the"}, {"pdf": 163, "printed": 136, "ch": 17, "text": "bank-level transmission from your own data; state clearly that the bridge is yours."}, {"pdf": 164, "printed": 137, "ch": 18, "text": "CHAPTER 18\n\nSynthesis: Four Readings, One Debate\n18.1 The shape of the debate\nThe four readings of this phase can be seen as attacks on, or defences of, a single calculation: what is the present value of the damage that emissions will cause, and how much should we pay now to avoid it? Figure 18.1 shows where each reading acts.\n\nEmissions path\nand warming\n\nDamage function\n(how much is lost)\n\nDietz–Stern:\nre-run DICE with\nendogenous growth\n\nDiscounting\n(how much it counts)\nStern (Ch. 2, Postscript):\nδ near zero; sensitivity\ntables\n\nRisk and tails\n(how bad, how likely)\nDietz–Stern also\nchange the damage\nfunction (convex) and\nadd climate risk\n\nWeitzman:\nthe tail can outweigh\nboth the discount rate\nand the mean damage\n\nGrowth and policy path\n(what is assumed)\n\nFigure 18.1. Where each reading acts on the cost-benefit calculation (my diagram). Stern’s Chapter 2 and Postscript work on the discount rate and show sensitivity; Weitzman works on risk and tails; Dietz and Stern re-run Nordhaus’s model with different growth, damage and risk assumptions.\n\n18.2 The Nordhaus–Stern debate in a paragraph\nThis paragraph is from general knowledge of the literature, not from the files; check before citing. Soon after the Stern Review appeared, Nordhaus and others argued that its strong conclusions followed mainly from its very low pure time preference rate, and that with a market-consistent rate the case would be for a gentler, rising path of emissions cuts. Stern’s replies are in the Postscript (Chapter 15): the ethical argument, and the tables showing that the loss remains above the cost of action even with a high δ. Weitzman’s paper then shifts the ground: even if the discount rate is agreed, the tail of the damage distribution can matter more. Dietz and Stern (2015) return to Nordhaus’s own model to show that changes in structure and risk, rather than ethics, can produce deep cuts. Three levers, then, are in play: discounting (ethics), the damage function and growth (structure), and the tail (uncertainty)."}, {"pdf": 165, "printed": 138, "ch": 18, "text": "Stern\nCh. 2\nPostscript\n\n+ Weitzman (2009)\n\nDietz and Stern (2015)\n\nType of paper\n\nEthics and method; sensi- Theory with a numerical il- Simulation study (read via tivity tables lustration summary)\n\nMain claim\n\nFuture generations count Under structural uncer- Nordhaus’s framework with\nequally; losses from inac- tainty and unbounded endogenous growth, convex\ntion exceed the cost of ac- exposure, the tail can dom- damages and climate risk tion across a wide range of inate expected cost-benefit supports deep early cuts δ and η\n\nWhat it varies\n\nδ, η, damage exponent\n\nTail shape, floor λ\n\nEvidence base\n\nPAGE2002 model runs\n\nStatistical argument; IPCC DICE-based simulations\nsensitivity studies\n\nMain weakness\n\nDepends on judgements Gives direction, not magni- Calibration; no adjustment\nthat cannot be tested\ntude\ncosts\n\nModel\nfixed\n\nstructure;\n\nethics\n\nTable 18.1. The three papers compared.\n\n18.3 What this phase gives the thesis\n1. A vocabulary for the assumptions behind any scenario: discount rate, damage function, tail risk, growth channel.\n2. A theoretical motivation for tail-based risk measures (Weitzman; Phase 5–6).\n3. A template for a sensitivity table (Stern’s PA.3).\n4. A set of plausible ranges for carbon prices (Dietz and Stern, subject to verification). What it does not give: any link to bank balance sheets. The bridge from carbon price and GDP shocks to capital ratios is the work of Phase 6 and of your own thesis.\n\n18.4 Corrections and gaps\nPlease read before relying on this phase\n\n• Dietz and Stern (2015) was read only via a web summary; all numbers need\nchecking (Chapter 17). Download the paper and I will replace the chapter.\n• The Postscript in your file is the 2006 postscript, not a 2009 text; Chapter 15 is labelled accordingly.\n• Weitzman (2009) came from your Layer 2 folder, not from the coursework folder.\n• Stern Chapter 6 (the model run that produced the headline numbers) is not\ncovered; Chapters 14–15 refer to it only as described in the Postscript.\n• Not in the plan, but in your Layer 2 folder: Nordhaus (DICE 2013), Pindyck\n(2013) and Weitzman (2007). They would round out the debate; say the word and\nI will add a short chapter."}, {"pdf": 166, "printed": 139, "ch": 18, "text": "• Open from Phase 2: Lavoie Chapter 6, Tirole Chapter 6, and the Nikolaidi papers.\n• Page numbers: Stern printed pages = PDF −45 for Chapter 2; the Postscript has none; Weitzman’s printed pages equal the PDF pages.\n\n18.5 A 90-minute lecture outline\nMinutes\n\nSegment\n\nContent and activity\n\n0–10\n\nHook\n\nThe two numbers: “≥ 5% of consumption” versus “1% of\nGDP”. Ask the class which is bigger and why the question\nis ill-posed.\n\n10–30\n\nEthics and discounting\n\nRamsey rule; thought experiment for η; survival table; Figure 14.1. Activity: each student picks δ and η and computes ρ and a present value.\n\n30–45\n\nSensitivity\n\nTables PA.2–PA.3; Figure 15.1. Activity: compute the ratios\nin the answer sketches.\n\n45–50\n\nBreak\n\n50–70\n\nFat tails\n\nNormal versus t; Figures 16.1–16.2; the 83% example.\n\n70–80\n\nStructure\n\nDietz and Stern: three modifications; caution that numbers\nare unverified.\n\n80–90\n\nDiscussion\n\nWhich lever (ethics, structure, tail) should a bank supervisor\ncare about most? Link to capital adequacy.\n\nTable 18.2. Suggested plan for teaching Phase 3. Adjust to your audience.\n\nTest: Phase 3\nRecall\nQ18.1 State the Ramsey rule and say which parameter Stern sets near zero.\nQ18.2 What does the Dismal Theorem say, in one sentence?\nExplain\nQ18.3 Why can a model with a small discount rate and a model with a large one both support strong action? (Use Table PA.3.)\nQ18.4 In what sense is the Weitzman result a statement about learning?\nApply\nQ18.5 With η = 2, a 2% chance of consumption falling to 2% of today’s, compute the sure payment (fraction of consumption) that gives the same expected utility as the gamble. Compare it with the expected loss.\nQ18.6 Rank the three levers (discounting, damage function, tail) by how directly each could be examined with bank data. Explain."}, {"pdf": 167, "printed": 140, "ch": 18, "text": "Viva\nQ18.7 “Climate economics is mostly ethics in disguise.” Evaluate using all three papers.\nQ18.8 Present, in five minutes, what a bank regulator should take from this phase and what it should not.\n\nAnswer sketches\nA18.1 ρ = δ + ηg; δ near zero (0.1%).\nA18.2 If tails are fat and exposure unbounded, the expected discount factor is not bounded, so the value of avoiding catastrophe can dominate everything else.\nA18.3 Because the loss falls with δ but remains above the cost of action even at 1.5% (Table PA.3); the conclusion is robust although the number is not.\nA18.4 An unknown scale means that data never fully resolves the tail; with only n observations the posterior is a Student-t, so learning thins the tail only at a power-law rate.\nA18.5 U = −1/C; floor 0.02 gives U = −50; EU = 0.98(−1) + 0.02(−50) = −1.98; 1 − w = 0.505; w ≈ 49.5%. Expected loss = 0.02 × 0.98 ≈ 2%; the payment is about 25 times larger.\nA18.6 Tail and damage function: partly, through loss distributions in the bank’s portfolio (Phase 5–6); discounting: hardly. A sensible ranking is tail > damage > discounting; the reasoning matters more than the order.\nA18.7 Evaluate the claim: Stern’s result moves with δ and η (ethics); Weitzman’s with λ and the tail (a value judgement on civilisation and an empirical unknown); Dietz–Stern with structure (partly empirical). A good answer says ethics is central in the first, partly in the second, and least in the third.\nA18.8 A good answer: take scenarios and tail measures; state the assumptions; do not take the point estimates as findings; link carbon price to credit losses through evidence on Indian borrowers."}], "cards": [{"id": "14.1", "ch": 14, "lv": "Recall", "q": "List the four features that make climate change a special kind of externality (p. 24–25).", "a": "Global, long-lasting, uncertain, non-marginal (large changes possible)."}, {"id": "14.2", "ch": 14, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the Ramsey rule and name each parameter. What are the values of δ and η in Stern’s main case?", "a": "ρ = δ + ηg. δ = 0.1% and η = 1 (Chapter 6, as quoted in the Postscript)."}, {"id": "14.3", "ch": 14, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are the two reasons the Review gives for discounting future consumption, and which does it accept?", "a": "Growth (future people are richer) and pure time preference (impatience, chance of nonexistence). It accepts the first, and the second only for the chance of non-existence."}, {"id": "14.4", "ch": 14, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does Stern quote from Ramsey, Pigou, Harrod and Solow? (One phrase each.)", "a": "Ramsey: “ethically indefensible”/“weakness of the imagination”. Pigou: “our telescopic faculty is defective”. Harrod: “a polite expression for rapacity and the conquest of reason by passion”. Solow: act as if the social rate of time preference were zero."}, {"id": "14.5", "ch": 14, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the SCC depend on the emissions path? Draw the idea of Figure 2.2.", "a": "More emissions mean higher temperatures; damages are convex, so the marginal damage of a tonne rises with the path. The price and the abatement are chosen together."}, {"id": "14.6", "ch": 14, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain why a δ of 1% a year is hard to defend if it is read as an extinction hazard.", "a": "e−0.01×100 = 0.368: a 63% chance of the end of the world in a century."}, {"id": "14.7", "ch": 14, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does “low δ” not imply “low ρ”? Use a numerical example.", "a": "ρ = δ + ηg; with δ = 0, η = 1.5, g = 2.5%: ρ = 3.75%."}, {"id": "14.8", "ch": 14, "lv": "Explain", "q": "In what sense does one parameter, η, do three jobs?", "a": "Inequality across people at one time, inequality across time (richer descendants), and aversion to risk."}, {"id": "14.9", "ch": 14, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain Stern’s claim that risk aversion need not be added to the discount rate.", "a": "The curvature of utility makes losses in bad states count more; expected utility already embodies that, so a further premium would double-count."}, {"id": "14.10", "ch": 14, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Compute ρ and the present value of 100 of damage 100 years from now, for (δ, η, g) = (0.1, 1, 2), (1.5, 1.5, 2) and (0.1, 2, 3). Use continuous discounting e−ρt .", "a": "(0.1, 1, 2): ρ = 2.1%, PV = 100e−2.1 = 12.2. (1.5, 1.5, 2): ρ = 4.5%, PV = 1.1. (0.1, 2, 3): ρ = 6.1%, PV = 100e−6.1 = 0.22. The same damage is worth about 11 times more in the first case than in the second, and 55 times more than in the third."}, {"id": "14.11", "ch": 14, "lv": "Apply", "q": "A bank stress test uses a 10-year horizon and a risk-free rate of 7%. The climate scenario you are given values damages using a 1.4% rate. Identify where the two rates differ and what each is for.", "a": "The 7% is a market rate used to value a financial flow over ten years; the 1.4% is a social rate built for consumption over centuries. They answer different questions, and one should not be substituted for the other without saying so."}, {"id": "14.12", "ch": 14, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Choose a number for η using Stern’s thought experiment: a poor person gains Rs X and a rich person (twice as rich) loses Rs Y . At what ratio Y /X do you call the change bad? What η does your answer imply?", "a": "Your own answer. If, say, you say Y may be up to twice X before you object (the rich person has twice the income), η = 1. If you object only when Y exceeds 4X, η = 2."}, {"id": "14.13", "ch": 14, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Pure time preference is an empirical fact about how people behave; Stern’s refusal to use it is paternalism.” Respond using both sides of the argument.", "a": "For: people do discount; ignoring this overrides preferences. Against (Stern): the question is not private choice but the weight that society places on people who cannot be represented in the decision; and the revealed rates differ widely (they depend on risk, liquidity, and constraints)."}, {"id": "14.14", "ch": 14, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“A thesis on Indian bank capital has no need to take sides on the Stern discount rate.” Agree or disagree, and say where in your thesis the choice would reappear.", "a": "Disagree, in part: the thesis can leave the long-run social discount rate aside, but the scenario’s carbon price path, and the choice of horizon in your capital analysis, embody it."}, {"id": "15.1", "ch": 15, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the Postscript’s three headline claims (cost of inaction, cost of action, businessas-usual outcome).", "a": "At least 5% (up to 20%) of consumption lost for ever; about 1% of GDP (plus or minus 3) to stabilise at 500–550 ppm CO2 e; above 850 ppm and a 50% chance of more than 5◦ C."}, {"id": "15.2", "ch": 15, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are the three groups of critics, and what is the one-line reply to each?", "a": "Science sceptics (the review follows the literature); wait and see (stock accumulates and some damage is irreversible); too low a δ (ethical argument and tables)."}, {"id": "15.3", "ch": 15, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the damage function of the annex and name its parameters.", "a": "Damage ∝ (TR /2.5)γ ; TR regional warming; γ triangular(1, 1.3, 3) in the baseline."}, {"id": "15.4", "ch": 15, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does the Postscript say about the status of integrated assessment models?", "a": "That they are “highly speculative” and that the analysis of disaggregated impacts should come first."}, {"id": "15.5", "ch": 15, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why do the “5%” and the “1%” not compare directly?", "a": "The first is a constant-equivalent loss in consumption from all future dates; the second is a yearly flow cost; the 1% also has a range of about 3 points either side."}, {"id": "15.6", "ch": 15, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does a higher η lower the loss in Table PA.2?", "a": "With higher η, a given loss falls on richer people (the future is richer) and counts for less."}, {"id": "15.7", "ch": 15, "lv": "Explain", "q": "What does the Hope (2006) reference show, and why does Stern cite it?", "a": "With δ = 2% the SCC is still about $40, just over half the central estimate: even a high δ leaves a high value."}, {"id": "15.8", "ch": 15, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does the damage exponent γ matter more than any data we have?", "a": "Because no observations exist at high temperatures; the choice between 1.3 and 3 changes the result by a factor of several and is not testable."}, {"id": "15.9", "ch": 15, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Using Table PA.3, compute the ratio of the loss at δ = 1.5 to the loss at δ = 0.1 for each of the six cases (low and high range, columns a, b, c). What is the smallest ratio and what does it say?", "a": "Low range: a 0.28, b 0.30, c 0.29. High range: a 0.28, b 0.28, c 0.27 (1.7/6.0, 4.0/14.2, 5.9/21.9). The smallest is about 0.27: raising δ fifteen-fold removes only about 70–73% of the loss."}, {"id": "15.10", "ch": 15, "lv": "Apply", "q": "For TR = 2.5 and TR = 5, compute (TR /2.5)γ for γ = 1, 1.3, 2.25, 3. Which γ would you defend and why?", "a": "TR = 5 gives 2γ : 2, 2.46, 4.76, 8; TR = 2.5 gives 1 in every case. A defence of any value must rest on the physical and economic literature on impacts, not on the model."}, {"id": "15.11", "ch": 15, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Design a one-page sensitivity table for your own thesis, in the style of PA.3: name the two parameters, the three or four values, and the outcome in each cell.", "a": "Example: rows δ and η, cells the estimated fall in the capital adequacy ratio. Any sensible answer names the scenario, the parameter values, and the unit."}, {"id": "15.12", "ch": 15, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“The Postscript is just a defence; the real finding is the original Review.” Reply.", "a": "The Postscript adds sensitivity tables and answers the critics; it does not change the model. A viva answer should say what it adds (the robustness) and what it does not (a new estimate)."}, {"id": "15.13", "ch": 15, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“If the loss stays above 1% for every δ, the choice of δ is irrelevant.” Evaluate.", "a": "Not irrelevant: the size of the loss varies by a factor of three or more (14.4 down to 4.2). It is irrelevant only to the sign of the policy conclusion; for the size of the carbon price the choice matters a great deal."}, {"id": "16.1", "ch": 16, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are Weitzman’s two ballpark figures for the bad tail of long-run warming (p. 2)?", "a": "Probability around 0.05 of warming above 10◦ C and around 0.01 of warming above 20◦ C in about two centuries."}, {"id": "16.2", "ch": 16, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Write the stochastic discount factor and the expected discount factor for normal Y . Which term is the precautionary term?", "a": "M = βe−ηY ; for normal Y , E[M ] = exp(−δ − ηµ + 12 η 2 s2 ), and the term in s2 is the precautionary term."}, {"id": "16.3", "ch": 16, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State Theorem 1. What are n, k, λ?", "a": "For any n, k: limλ→∞ E[M |λ] = +∞. n is the number of observations, k the exponent of the prior on the scale, λ the VSL-like parameter."}, {"id": "16.4", "ch": 16, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does the consumption floor D(λ) equal for η = 2, λ = 100 and 1000?", "a": "0.01 and 0.001, from D = [1 + (η − 1)λ]−1/(η−1) ."}, {"id": "16.5", "ch": 16, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does an unknown scale produce a fat-tailed posterior-predictive distribution?", "a": "Averaging over the possible scales puts weight on very wide distributions, whose tails dominate; the mixture is a Student-t with a power-law tail. 2 2"}, {"id": "16.6", "ch": 16, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain nonuniform convergence with Figure 16.2.", "a": "For the normal the curve flattens at eη s /2 = 7.4; for the t it keeps rising, so the result depends on where one cuts the tail."}, {"id": "16.7", "ch": 16, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is a damage function that is accurate up to 3◦ C of little help in the argument?", "a": "Because the theorem concerns the region beyond the data, which the function does not constrain; see Figure 16.3."}, {"id": "16.8", "ch": 16, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why is the Dismal Theorem not an impossibility result?", "a": "It says that the model with unbounded exposure gives no finite answer; the answer depends on λ and the tail, so judgement is needed (the rule of reason)."}, {"id": "16.9", "ch": 16, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Contrast the role of η in Stern and in Weitzman.", "a": "Stern: η governs inequality and risk aversion, and a higher η lowers the loss because the future is richer. Weitzman: η > 1 is needed for the floor, and a higher η raises the weight on disaster."}, {"id": "16.10", "ch": 16, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Redo the willingness-to-pay example with η = 2, a catastrophe probability of 1% and a floor D = 0.01. What fraction of consumption would you pay? Compare it with the expected loss.", "a": "D = 0.01, so U (D) = −100. Expected utility = 0.99(−1) + 0.01(−100) = −1.99. 1 − w = 1/1.99 = 0.50: w ≈ 50%. Expected loss = 0.01 × 0.99 ≈ 1%. The gap is a factor of about 50."}, {"id": "16.11", "ch": 16, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Using Table 16.1, how many times more likely is a 5-standard-deviation loss under the t than under the normal? What does that imply for a bank that sets capital with a normal model?", "a": "1.06 × 10−3 /2.87 × 10−7 ≈ 3,700. The bank would hold far too little capital against an event it regards as nearly impossible."}, {"id": "16.12", "ch": 16, "lv": "Apply", "q": "You must write one paragraph for a regulator summarising what Weitzman implies for a climate stress test. Draft it.", "a": "A sound answer: use scenarios and tail measures, state the assumed tail openly, run sensitivity on the floor/cutoff, and do not present a single capital number as if it were precise."}, {"id": "16.13", "ch": 16, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Weitzman shows that cost-benefit analysis is impossible for climate; so use the precautionary principle.” Evaluate.", "a": "The Dismal Theorem implies that a finite cutoff is not guaranteed to give a good approximation, so cost-benefit numbers should be presented with their dependence on assumptions; it does not say CBA is impossible or that the precautionary principle mechanically decides."}, {"id": "16.14", "ch": 16, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Fat-tailed climate risk is no different from the fat tails already in credit portfolios.” Agree or disagree.", "a": "Partly agree: both have fat tails and the statistics are similar. Disagree on what can be learned: a bank has data on many loans and years (the n is large and the structure repeated), while climate has one planet; the learning that would thin the tail is not available."}, {"id": "17.1", "ch": 17, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What are the three modifications Dietz and Stern make to DICE?", "a": "Endogenous growth (capital and TFP versions), convex damages (calibrated to 50% at 6◦ C, with a high case at 4◦ C), and stochastic climate sensitivity."}, {"id": "17.2", "ch": 17, "lv": "Recall", "q": "Which parameters of DICE do they leave unchanged?", "a": "The social parameters: pure time preference 1.5% and η = 1.5 (check)."}, {"id": "17.3", "ch": 17, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the optimal 2015 control rates reported for standard DICE and for the two families of new models. (Check the numbers.)", "a": "0.158; 0.213–0.342; 0.272–0.396."}, {"id": "17.4", "ch": 17, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why does endogenous growth make climate damage more costly than in DICE?", "a": "Damage reduces investment and so the growth of knowledge and productivity; the loss compounds."}, {"id": "17.5", "ch": 17, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why do the authors emphasise that they leave the discounting at DICE values?", "a": "So that the result cannot be attributed to the ethical choice that divides Stern and Nordhaus."}, {"id": "17.6", "ch": 17, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Explain how a stochastic climate sensitivity raises the optimal cut even if the mean sensitivity is unchanged.", "a": "Because of the convexity of damage, the high-sensitivity states cost more than the low-sensitivity states save; the expected marginal damage rises."}, {"id": "17.7", "ch": 17, "lv": "Explain", "q": "What does the absence of adjustment costs imply for the interpretation of the results?", "a": "Smooth adjustment is assumed; rapid cuts could cost more, so the results may overstate the case for a fast start."}, {"id": "17.8", "ch": 17, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Convert $44 and $233 per tonne of carbon to per tonne of CO2 .", "a": "About $12 and $63 per tonne of CO2 (44/3.67 and 233/3.67)."}, {"id": "17.9", "ch": 17, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Model A: a one-off 5% fall in the level of output. Model B: growth is 0.5 points lower (1.5% instead of 2%) for 10 years. Compare the output gaps after 10 years. Then compare them if B’s lower growth lasts 50 years.", "a": "Model A: output is 5% below baseline for ever. Model B after 10 years: (1.015/1.02)10 = 0.952, a gap of about 4.8%, so close to A. If the slower growth lasts 50 years the gap is 1 − (1.015/1.02)50 ≈ 22%. Lesson: a growth effect is far worse than a level effect if it persists."}, {"id": "17.10", "ch": 17, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“A model that reaches deep cuts by changing only the structure gives more credible support than one that reaches them by changing the ethics.” Discuss.", "a": "A balanced answer: structural changes can be debated on evidence, whereas ethics cannot be tested; but the structural changes are also calibrated judgements (the convexity of damage), so credibility is a matter of degree."}, {"id": "17.11", "ch": 17, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Because the paper is a calibrated simulation, it says nothing about Indian banks.” How would you use it responsibly?", "a": "Use it for the price range of carbon and for the direction of the effect; then build the bank-level transmission from your own data; state clearly that the bridge is yours."}, {"id": "18.1", "ch": 18, "lv": "Recall", "q": "State the Ramsey rule and say which parameter Stern sets near zero.", "a": "ρ = δ + ηg; δ near zero (0.1%)."}, {"id": "18.2", "ch": 18, "lv": "Recall", "q": "What does the Dismal Theorem say, in one sentence?", "a": "If tails are fat and exposure unbounded, the expected discount factor is not bounded, so the value of avoiding catastrophe can dominate everything else."}, {"id": "18.3", "ch": 18, "lv": "Explain", "q": "Why can a model with a small discount rate and a model with a large one both support strong action? (Use Table PA.3.)", "a": "Because the loss falls with δ but remains above the cost of action even at 1.5% (Table PA.3); the conclusion is robust although the number is not."}, {"id": "18.4", "ch": 18, "lv": "Explain", "q": "In what sense is the Weitzman result a statement about learning?", "a": "An unknown scale means that data never fully resolves the tail; with only n observations the posterior is a Student-t, so learning thins the tail only at a power-law rate."}, {"id": "18.5", "ch": 18, "lv": "Apply", "q": "With η = 2, a 2% chance of consumption falling to 2% of today’s, compute the sure payment (fraction of consumption) that gives the same expected utility as the gamble. Compare it with the expected loss.", "a": "U = −1/C; floor 0.02 gives U = −50; EU = 0.98(−1) + 0.02(−50) = −1.98; 1 − w = 0.505; w ≈ 49.5%. Expected loss = 0.02 × 0.98 ≈ 2%; the payment is about 25 times larger."}, {"id": "18.6", "ch": 18, "lv": "Apply", "q": "Rank the three levers (discounting, damage function, tail) by how directly each could be examined with bank data. Explain.", "a": "Tail and damage function: partly, through loss distributions in the bank’s portfolio (Phase 5–6); discounting: hardly. A sensible ranking is tail > damage > discounting; the reasoning matters more than the order."}, {"id": "18.7", "ch": 18, "lv": "Viva", "q": "“Climate economics is mostly ethics in disguise.” Evaluate using all three papers.", "a": "Evaluate the claim: Stern’s result moves with δ and η (ethics); Weitzman’s with λ and the tail (a value judgement on civilisation and an empirical unknown); Dietz–Stern with structure (partly empirical). A good answer says ethics is central in the first, partly in the second, and least in the third."}, {"id": "18.8", "ch": 18, "lv": "Viva", "q": "Present, in five minutes, what a bank regulator should take from this phase and what it should not.", "a": "A good answer: take scenarios and tail measures; state the assumptions; do not take the point estimates as findings; link carbon price to credit losses through evidence on Indian borrowers."}]};